nt; use a THREE.Line? A ribbon: BufferGeometry with 2 verts per segment updated each frame — doable and cheap. 8 segments, width 0.5. Material: MeshBasicMaterial double side, dark red. This looks great at speed.)\n\nsrc/player.ts — physics state machine + rope + claw + input handling + camera control + FOV/shake. Also spawn & checkpoint respawn.\n\nsrc/course.ts — rings (torus + pillars + glow), coins (small cylinders rotating), pass detection, target arrow (3D cone above hero? or HUD arrow), next-ring distance.\n\nsrc/hud.ts — DOM bindings: speed, alt, timer, coins, combo, best, banner messages, result overlay.\n\nsrc/main.ts — bootstrap: renderer, scene, camera, lights, loading screen wiring, start button, game loop, state (loading/title/playing/result), resize, tiles update, update order.\n\nindex.html — full UI + styles (dark ninja aesthetic, Japanese font stack).\n\nLet me write all the files now. I'll be careful about correctness (three r160 API: CapsuleGeometry exists since r140 ✓; outputColorSpace; SRGBColorSpace ✓; `renderer.useLegacyLights` default false in r155+ — light intensities need care: prototype used DirectionalLight(0.7) etc with MeshStandardMaterial — in r160, physically correct lighting is default; intensities like 0.7 for directional are OK-ish. Prototype was presumably verified in browser, keep same values.\n\nGround circle: CircleGeometry with quaternion from (0,0,1) to UP — keep.\n\nOne issue in prototype: `ray.firstHitOnly = true` — Raycaster.firstHitOnly exists in r160? It was added in r163 I think. Harmless ('firstHitOnly' in ray check).\n\nThree import paths for GLTFLoader/DRACOLoader: 'three/examples/jsm/loaders/GLTFLoader.js' — with npm three 0.160 + Vite, resolves fine.\n\nLet me now write files. Keep code quality high, no comments (per instructions! \"DO NOT ADD ANY COMMENTS unless asked\"). Hmm — that's for code comments. The instruction is explicit: don't add comments. I'll write clean code without comments.\n\nLet me write src/config.ts first.\n\nCourse checkpoint coords (lat, lon, alt meters above ground/sea):\n- start: 35.0033, 135.7598 (四条烏丸)\n- CP2: 34.9995 (烏丸丸太町? ≈34.9988)... let me distribute evenly-ish along 烏丸通: 四条(35.0033) → 京都駅(34.9858). Real stations along Karasuma subway: 丸太町 (35.0012? south of 御池? Actually subway Karasuma line stations south from 四条: 烏丸御池(35.0008? north), 四条(35.0033), 五条(34.9955), 七条? no — 七条 is not Karasuma... Subway stations: 四条 (35.0033), 五条 (34.9956), 七条? Actually Karasuma line: 十条, 九条, 七条? Let me recall: 京都駅(34.9858)→ 五条(34.9956) → 四条(35.0033) → 烏丸御池 → 丸太町 → 今出川... South of 五条: 七条(34.9909)? Hmm, Karasuma Line stations: 国際会館... 北大路, 鞍馬口, 今出川, 丸太町, 烏丸御池, 四条, 五条, 竹田 (via 七条? no). Wait, there's 九条? The Kintetsu/Hankyu? Hmm.\n\nActually Karasuma Subway from north to south: 国際会館, 松ヶ崎, 北山, 北大路, 鞍馬口, 今出川, 丸太町, 烏丸御池, 四条, 五条, 竹田 (express?), with 七条? No — between 五条 and 竹田 there's 七条? Hmm, no: 五条 → 竹田 has intermediate \"七条\"? I don't think Karasuma line has 七条 station... Hmm, it does NOT; but 七条通 crosses 烏丸通 at ~34.9907.\n\nWhatever — exact station names don't matter. Ring labels: 四条烏丸 (START), 五条, 七条, 八条, 京都駅 (GOAL). Use real 七条/八条通 coordinates along Karasuma ~34.9907 / ~34.9886.\n\nVerify 八条通: 京都駅 north side is 八条通 (station sits on 八条通/東山? Kyoto Station main entrance faces 八条通? Actually 京都駅's south side is 八条口? The station building is between 八条通 (north?) hmm. 八条通 runs along the station's north side? Kyoto station is bounded by 七条通 (north), 八条通? No — Kyoto Station is directly south of 七条通, north of 八条通? Hmm: the station address is 八条口 (Hachijo-guchi) south side... The 京都駅ビル spans from 七条通 (north edge) to 八条通? I recall 烏丸中央口 is on the 八条通 side? Not exactly; the station is between 七条 and 八条通... The building occupies the block north of 八条通... I'll place goal ring above the station center (34.9858, 135.7588) which lies between 七条 and 八条 anyway. Ring labels: 2=五条烏丸, 3=七条烏丸, 4=八条烏丸 — all plausible street names along 烏丸通. Good enough for a game.\n\nRing heights: CP1 150, CP2 140, CP3 130, CP4 120, GOAL 170 (京都駅ビル is tall ~60-130m; ring above at 170 visible).\n\nCoins: between rings, 8-12 coins each segment, following a swing arc: interpolate lat/lon along the corridor, height sinusoid between ring heights ±30, plus lateral spread. Total ~50 coins.\n\nSpawn: at CP1 ring, 160m, initial velocity south (toward 五条) at 25 m/s.\n\nAlso \"逆走 allowed\" — free roam, rings just sequential.\n\nDistances: CP1→CP2 ≈ 0.0077°lat ≈ 855m; CP2→CP3 ≈ 0.0049 ≈ 543m; CP3→CP4 ≈ 0.0021 ≈ 233m; CP4→GOAL ≈ 0.0028 ≈ 311m. Total ≈ 1.94km. \n\nTime bonus per checkpoint: +5s? Score 1000 each. Koban 100.\n\nFall handling: if player's altitude above ground plane (approximate: use ring last checkpoint height? simpler: absolute altitude from ECEF radius minus earth radius ~ 34.99 lat → h above ellipsoid) < 25m → fall event: respawn at 40m above last checkpoint with zero velocity, penalty: +3s added to timer? Keep simple: 3 second penalty.\n\nAlso \"接地しても即死せず、ビルの壁を登る?\" Too much. Fall → respawn.\n\nR key: full restart.\n\nNow, second thought on tilt: Should the game be auto-grapple friendly? Prototype autoMode default true — good.\n\nWrite the code now. Start: tsconfig.json, vite.config.ts, index.html, then src files.\n\nFor maintainability I'll write substantial files in one Write each. Let me draft mentally then write.\n\n--- src/geo.ts\n```ts\nimport * as THREE from 'three';\n\nconst A = 6378137;\nconst E2 = 6.69437999014e-3;\n\nexport function lonLatToEcef(latD: number, lonD: number, h = 0): THREE.Vector3 {\n  const lat = THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(latD);\n  const lon = THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(lonD);\n  const N = A / Math.sqrt(1 - E2 * Math.sin(lat) ** 2);\n  return new THREE.Vector3(\n    (N + h) * Math.cos(lat) * Math.cos(lon),\n    (N + h) * Math.cos(lat) * Math.sin(lon),\n    (N * (1 - E2) + h) * Math.sin(lat),\n  );\n}\n\nexport class Frame {\n  readonly origin: THREE.Vector3;\n  readonly east: THREE.Vector3;\n  readonly north: THREE.Vector3;\n  readonly up: THREE.Vector3;\n  constructor(readonly lat: number, readonly lon: number, readonly h = 0) {\n    this.origin = lonLatToEcef(lat, lon, h);\n    this.up = this.origin.clone().normalize();\n    this.east = new THREE.Vector3().crossVectors(new THREE.Vector3(0, 0, 1), this.up).normalize();\n    this.north = new THREE.Vector3().crossVectors(this.up, this.east).normalize();\n  }\n  at(lat: number, lon: number, h: number, out = new THREE.Vector3()): THREE.Vector3 {\n    const w = lonLatToEcef(lat, lon, h);\n    const lo = lonLatToEcef(lat, lon, 0); // project onto local frame: w - origin, decompose\n    const d = w.clone().sub(this.origin);\n    return out.set(\n      d.dot(this.east), d.dot(this.up), -d.dot(this.north)  // local: x=east, y=up, z=-north (north = -z)\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm — I need to decide ONE convention: game logic in local meters (x=east, y=up, z=north?) or pure ECEF? \n\nDecision: **local meters, z = south-positive?** Let me define local frame: x = east, y = up, z = -north (so increasing z = going south — matching prototype corridor south). Then a root group holds local-frame objects; camera gets placed in local coords and I convert camera to ECEF each frame by applying frame transform: `camera.position.copy(local→ECEF)`, and camera quaternion needs basis alignment too (camera up = UP). Simplest robust approach: **everything local**, including camera as a child of the env root group:\n\n```\nscene (ECEF)\n├── env (Object3D, matrix = [east, up, -north, ORIGIN])\n│   ├── camera (local position) — camera.matrixWorld auto-computed in ECEF\n│   ├── hero, rope, rings, coins, street, fallback city\n├── tiles (ECEF)\n├── stars/moon (ECEF)\n```\n\nTilesRenderer setCamera(camera): uses camera.matrixWorld → ECEF ✓. Raycast against tiles: raycaster in world space ✓. Raycaster vs local objects: three handles via matrixWorld ✓. So `ray.intersectObject(heroParent...)` works with mixed frames as long as objects' matrixWorld are updated.\n\nOne catch: renderer render(scene, camera) with camera child of env — fine.\n\nSo: local frame everywhere except tiles/stars/moon. Conversions: `toWorld(localVec3)` → ECEF vector (for HUD distances use local directly). \n\nenv matrix: `env.matrixAutoUpdate = false; env.matrix.makeBasis(east, up, back=north.clone().negate()); env.position.copy(ORIGIN)`. Note makeBasis sets columns; local (x,y,z) maps to (east, up, -north). Decompose direction: worldDir = east*lx + up*ly - north*lz.\n\nFor lighting direction (sun): DirectionalLight direction is in parent space of its target — sun is in scene (ECEF). Set sun.position = ORIGIN + up*3000, target ORIGIN. Fine.\n\nCamera up vector: camera.up is used by lookAt — set camera.up.set(0,1,0) in local frame → maps to UP world ✓.\n\nOK this is clean. All gameplay math in local coords with Y up. Hero, rings etc. straightforward three.js.\n\nviewDir local: standard spherical from yaw/pitch: (cos(pitch)*sin(yaw), sin(pitch), -cos(pitch)*cos(yaw))? Let me define yaw=0 → facing -z (south, toward 京都駅). Facing dir = (sin(yaw)*cos(pitch), sin(pitch), -cos(yaw)*cos(pitch)). Initial yaw = 0 faces south. \n\nRight vector = cross(fwd, up) = straightforward in local.\n\nNow the raycaster for grapple: ray.setFromCamera(center, camera) → world ECEF ray; intersect tiles group (ECEF) and also fallback/street objects (local, under env). intersectObject(t.group, true) works because it uses matrixWorld of both. ✓\n\nCourse/coins local coords via frame.at(lat, lon, h) returning Vector3.\n\nCheckpoint pass detection: for ring with facing normal n (ring axis along local x? make ring face along the corridor direction), compute player local pos minus ring center; pass if within radius 20 along-axis AND within ring radius in plane. Simpler: distance from ring center < R_ring*0.9 in 3D? A player flying through a 25m torus: use center distance < 22 → too generous horizontally? Torus ring radius 25, tube 2. Passing through hole means lateral distance from the ring axis < 22 AND crossing the plane. I'll implement: axis = direction from previous checkpoint to this one (normalized local). Crossing = sign change of dot(p-center, axis) since last frame, plus lateral distance = (p-center) - proj < 20. That's proper.\n\nGoal ring bigger (radius 40).\n\nArrow indicator: HUD shows direction text? A 3D floating cone at next ring + a screen-space arrow. Simplest: a HUD \"→ 1.2km\" plus a 3D beacon (glowing column of light at ring). I'll add beacon: cylinder additive glow at ring, visible from afar. \n\nRing visuals: torii-like gate: two red pillars + top beam, plus a translucent glowing torus in the middle. Build from boxes. Label sprite with canvas text (四条烏丸 etc.) — sprites with Japanese text via canvas texture. Nice touch for presentation!\n\nCoins: small gold cylinders (koban) rotating, additive sparkle sprite.\n\nNinja scarf ribbon: implement in ninja.ts.\n\nLet me now also plan HUD/DOM:\n\n- #ui-loading (progress bar)\n- #ui-title (start card with instructions, PLATEAU attribution)\n- #hud (speed km/h, alt, timer, coins, combo, best)\n- #banner (checkpoint messages, animated)\n- #ui-result (time/score/record + restart)\n- #cross (reticle, red when grapple available)\n- #speed-fx (vignette + speed lines)\n\nState machine: loading (tiles arrive or fallback) → title → playing → result (goal) ; R restart; fall → banner + respawn.\n\nTimer starts on game start; stops at goal.\n\nOK. Also mobile: touch hold+release + drag look. Keep prototype's.\n\nNow write it all. Order: tsconfig, vite.config, index.html, geo, config, audio, plateau, street, ninja, course, player, hud, main.\n\nLet me think about key gameplay tuning (important for fun):\n- GRAV 26 m/s² at real scale... real gravity is 9.8. Game feel: use ~22 with buoyant drag. Swing arc radius up to 260m. Speed target 45-70 m/s (160-250 km/h). Drag: multiply 1-0.04*dt (linear damping) fine.\n- Pump: +22 m/s along view when rope taut (prototype) — good.\n- Release boost: *1.6 + up 12 — strong. Maybe *1.35 + up 8 to keep control.\n- Reel in: -30 m/s rope length while hold.\n- Auto assist: every 0.35s if falling & speed>4.\n\nCamera: follow behind: pos = player - fwd*7 + up*3, lerp fast; lookAt player + fwd*14 + vel*0.2; FOV 62→100 by speed; shake at high speed.\n\nGround plane: at h≈0 local y=0 — fall if y < 25.\n\nBut Kyoto Station building is tall; \"y<25\" means hitting streets — with buildings in the way, rope raycast hits buildings; falling player will hit a building — no building collision (raycast only for grapple). Player passes through buildings visually (ghost). Hmm, that breaks immersion but ok for hackathon? Better: add simple building collision: raycast downward from player each frame? Expensive. Alternative: sample terrain height by raycasting down every 0.2s (tiles) — moderate. \n\nCompromise: falling below y<30 triggers \"転倒\" respawn regardless. Visual passing-through is a minor issue. Actually we could make it nicer: when falling toward a building it's fine to pass through (you're a ninja ghost? no). For polish, do a cheap check: raycast from player down (0,-1) against scene every 0.15s; if hit within 2m below feet... eh, tiles raycast cost. I'll do the simple y-threshold.\n\nOne more mechanic for depth: **wall run / building bounce?** Skip — scope.\n\nDash (Shift): short forward burst with cooldown (ninja smoke puff). Adds game feel. Add: Shift = 煙玉ダッシュ (smoke dash) — 25 m/s forward impulse + burst particle + puff sound, 2s cooldown. Fun, cheap.\n\nLet me write the code now. I'll aim for quality + compactness.\n\nAlso: PLATEAU attribution in UI (required by license): \"3D都市モデル 国土交通省 PLATEAU (京都市 2025 LOD2)\".\n\nNow write files.\n\nvite.config.ts: no cesium plugin; base: './' (relative, for CraftStadium subpath safety). server port 5173.\n\ntsconfig.json: standard vite tsconfig.\n\nLet me write index.html with full UI + CSS (ninja aesthetic: black/red/white, Japanese gothic fonts).\n\nAudio module details:\n\n```ts\nexport class Audio {\n  ctx: AudioContext | null = null;\n  master: GainNode\n  windGain, windFilter, windSrc\n  musicGain, delay\n  enabled\n  init() on user gesture\n  setSpeed(sp) → windGain target = clamp((sp-8)/60, 0, 1) * .35, filter freq 300+sp*18\n  blip(freq, dur, type, gain) helper\n  noise(dur, filterType, f0, f1, gain)\n  coin() two sine blips 1046.5, 1568\n  attach() noise burst lowpass 800 + sine 180 thump\n  whoosh() bandpass sweep 400→2200, dur .35\n  checkpoint() koto plucks: pentatonic notes\n  goal() arpeggio\n  fall() saw 400→80\n  dash() noise mid\n  startMusic() pentatonic generative loop every 250ms lookahead scheduling\n}\n```\n\nBGM: koto-like: triangle osc + quick decay, notes from scale [D3, F3, G3, A3, C4, D4, F4, G4, A4], random walk with delay feedback (feedback delay .3s, wet .25). Plus a soft taiko-like low thump every beat (sine 80Hz decay .25 at 90bpm eighth schedule). Good enough.\n\nNow writing. This is a lot of code; let me get to it. I'll write each file with the Write tool.\n\nFirst: tsconfig.json + vite.config.ts.\n\nThen src/geo.ts:\n\n```ts\nimport * as THREE from 'three';\n\nconst A = 6378137;\nconst E2 = 6.69437999014e-3;\n\nexport function lonLatToEcef(latD: number, lonD: number, h = 0): THREE.Vector3 {\n  const lat = THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(latD);\n  const lon = THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(lonD);\n  const s = Math.sin(lat);\n  const N = A / Math.sqrt(1 - E2 * s * s);\n  return new THREE.Vector3(\n    (N + h) * Math.cos(lat) * Math.cos(lon),\n    (N + h) * Math.cos(lat) * Math.sin(lon),\n    (N * (1 - E2) + h) * s,\n  );\n}\n\nexport class LocalFrame {\n  readonly origin: THREE.Vector3;\n  readonly east: THREE.Vector3;\n  readonly north: THREE.Vector3;\n  readonly up: THREE.Vector3;\n\n  constructor(lat: number, lon: number, h = 0) {\n    this.origin = lonLatToEcef(lat, lon, h);\n    this.up = this.origin.clone().normalize();\n    this.east = new THREE.Vector3().crossVectors(new THREE.Vector3(0, 0, 1), this.up).normalize();\n    this.north = new THREE.Vector3().crossVectors(this.up, this.east).normalize();\n  }\n\n  toWorld(p: THREE.Vector3, out = new THREE.Vector3()): THREE.Vector3 {\n    return out.copy(this.origin)\n      .addScaledVector(this.east, p.x)\n      .addScaledVector(this.up, p.y)\n      .addScaledVector(this.north, -p.z);\n  }\n\n  geo(lat: number, lon: number, h: number, out = new THREE.Vector3()): THREE.Vector3 {\n    const w = lonLatToEcef(lat, lon, h);\n    const d = w.sub(this.origin);\n    return out.set(d.dot(this.east), d.dot(this.up), -d.dot(this.north));\n  }\n}\n```\n\nsrc/config.ts:\n\n```ts\nexport const PLATEAU_TILESETS = [\n  'https://assets.cms.plateau.reearth.io/assets/f0/2ed501-5480-4ccb-8293-9469a3856f9a/26100_kyoto-shi_city_2025_citygml_1_op_bldg_3dtiles_26106_shimogyo-ku_lod2/tileset.json',\n  'https://assets.cms.plateau.reearth.io/assets/04/eaac56-936e-49e2-a91b-0db31a6ed192/26100_kyoto-shi_city_2025_citygml_1_op_bldg_3dtiles_26104_nakagyo-ku_lod2/tileset.json'\n];\n```\n\nWait — need to double check these tileset URLs are current/valid. The first one verified 200 via the proxy (which is just passthrough). Let me verify the direct CMS URL works (it did — the CORS HEAD check returned 200 from the direct URL!). ✓ Second one (nakagyo) — verify too.\n\nAlso check: tileset root + children reference relative b3dm — relative URLs resolved against the tileset URL → same host → CORS ok.\n\nFallback timeout 15s.\n\nFRAME origin: lat 34.9945, lon 135.7585 (between 四条 and 駅).\n\nCourse:\n\n```ts\nexport interface Checkpoint { lat: number; lon: number; h: number; label: string; goal?: boolean }\nexport const COURSE: Checkpoint[] = [\n  { label: '四条烏丸', lat: 35.00330, lon: 135.75980, h: 150 },\n  { label: '五条烏丸', lat: 34.99550, lon: 135.75920, h: 140 },\n  { label: '七条烏丸', lat: 34.99090, lon: 135.75870, h: 125 },\n  { label: '八条烏丸', lat: 34.98850, lon: 135.75850, h: 120 },\n  { label: '京都駅', lat: 34.98580, lon: 135.75880, h: 175, goal: true },\n];\n```\n\nSPAWN: above CP1 at 175: COURSE[0] with h 175, facing south, initial speed 30.\n\nGRAVITY 24, MAX_ROPE 280, etc.\n\nTUNING consts in config or player. Put in config.\n\nNow writing plateau.ts:\n\n```ts\nexport interface TilesHandle { renderers: any[]; group: THREE.Group; fallbackCity: THREE.Group; setDayNight(night: boolean); dispose() }\n\nexport function createPlateau(scene, camera, renderer, onStatus: (msg: string, loaded: boolean) => void)\n```\n\n- DRACOLoader path 'draco/'\n- GLTFLoader with draco\n- for each URL: TilesRenderer, addHandler(/\\.gltf$/, gltfLoader)?? wait — the prototype registers gltfLoader for .gltf requests. In 0.3.34 TilesRenderer accepts a `loader` option? It used `t.manager.addHandler(/\\.gltf$/, gltfLoader)`; keep same approach (manager property exists — LoadingManager). Let me check TilesRenderer.js quickly for `manager` property. It extends... In 0.3.x, TilesRenderer has `this.manager = new LoadingManager()`? Let me grep.\n\nAlso `setCamera`, `setResolutionFromRenderer`, `group`, `update()` — verify in 0.3.34 TilesRenderer.js.\n\nLet me grep the package quickly before writing modules.",
