          "text": "Confirmed: local lib and npm 0.3.34 are identical (same hash), and there is **no 'load-content' or 'load-error' event** in 0.3.34. The correct events are:\n- `load-tile-set` (root tileset URL loaded successfully)\n- `load-model` (a tile's model loaded)\n- `tile-visibility-change`\n\nFor error detection: TilesRenderer likely logs errors via `this.onTilesLoaded`... Let me check how failures are reported. In 0.3.34, `loadTileSet` catches errors? Let me check around line 348 (load-tile-set dispatch) for try/catch and 'error' events. Actually simpler: for my implementation I'll use:\n- 'load-tile-set' → tileset fetched, ready to load content\n- 'load-model' → one more building batch loaded (count for progress)\n- Also I can wrap the initial fetch myself: `fetch(tilesetUrl)` to check accessibility, with timeout → fallback.\n\nFor robustness I'll track readiness via:\n1. My own HEAD/GET probe of both tileset URLs (fetch with timeout 8s) — if both fail → fallback immediately.\n2. 'load-tile-set' fired → mark tileset OK.\n3. 'load-model' count for progress.\n4. A failsafe: if player has been playing 20s and no 'load-model' → keep building (fallback stays as extra scaffolding, that's fine — actually the fallback would double with real tiles; but they're in different positions... the fallback grid would intersect real buildings! The prototype builds fallback at 12s even if tiles later arrive — visual mess.\n\nBetter design: fallback only if tiles fail (probe fails or no load-tile-set within 10s). And the fallback uses a *different location*?? No — same corridor, but without real tiles it looks similar. If real tiles arrive after fallback built, hide fallback group (fallbackGroup.visible = false) — safe.\n\nActually, since the probe tells us within ~2-5s whether tilesets are reachable, and 'load-tile-set' fires quickly, use: probe → fallback decision at ~8s. Load timeout: if 'load-tile-set' hasn't fired for a renderer within 8s, mark that renderer failed (but