          "messageID": "msg_124c41c4f001wHpkLetlZCU60F",
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Chrome and Edge are both installed. I'll drive Chrome via the Chrome DevTools Protocol with plain node (no npm install) — using `chrome.exe --headless=new --remote-debugging-port` and a minimal CDP client over WebSocket. Node 24 has a built-in WebSocket client (`WebSocket` global, stable since 22)! So I can write a small CDP script with zero dependencies:\n\n1. Launch chrome headless with remote debugging port + user data dir (temp).\n2. fetch http://127.0.0.1:PORT/json to get target ws URL.\n3. Connect WebSocket, send Runtime.enable, Log.enable, Page.enable, Console.enable, then Page.navigate to the preview URL.\n4. Collect Runtime.consoleAPICalled, Runtime.exceptionThrown, Log.entryAdded, Network errors for ~25s.\n5. Evaluate expressions: window state, e.g. check `document.querySelector('#ui-title')` visibility, simulate click on start, check phase... Phase is module-scoped; expose some debug hooks? I can query DOM: after load, #ui-title should be visible (after probe + first content or 7s). Then click #btn-start, wait 3s, check #hud visible and #hud-time text != 0:00.00. Also evaluate `renderer.info` not accessible... simpler DOM checks + console errors.\n\nFor WebGL in headless chrome: use flags `--use-angle=swiftshader` (software GL) or `--enable-unsafe-swiftshader`. New headless supports WebGL via SwiftShader with `--use-angle=swiftshader-webgl`? The standard flags for headless WebGL: `--headless=new --use-gl=angle --use-angle=swiftshader`. Chrome 120+ needs `--enable-unsafe-swiftshader` to allow software WebGL. Let me include those.\n\nTiles will actually download during the test — headless chrome can access the network (it's the same machine). \n\nPlan:\n- Start `vite preview` (dist) on port 4173.\n- Run node script to verify.\n\nLet me write the CDP script in the temp dir.",
