          "text": "Now the game boots much further. The TDZ error is fixed. New bug: `updateScarf` — `Float32Array.set` offset out of bounds. \n\nLooking at my scarf code:\n```ts\nconst posAttr = this.scarf.geometry.getAttribute('position') as THREE.BufferAttribute;\nconst arr = posAttr.array as Float32Array;\n...\narr.set([a.x + side.x, a.y + side.y, a.z + side.z, a.x - side.x, a.y - side.y, a.z - side.z, b.x - side.x, b.y - side.y, b.z - side.z], i * 6);\n```\nThe array has (scarfSegs-1)*6 = 9*6 = 54 floats. Last i = 8 → offset 48, +9 values = 57 > 54! I write 9 values per segment (wrong — should be 6: a+side, a-side, b-side). I intended 2 verts per point + 2 for next = 4 verts per segment? But index buffer references i*4..i*4+3 (4 verts per segment = 12 floats). So position attribute needs (scarfSegs-1)*12 = 108 floats. Mismatch: UV/index built for 4 verts/segment (12 floats) but `pos` Float32Array created with (scarfSegs-1)*6.\n\nFix: pos = new Float32Array((scarfSegs-1)*12), and write 4 verts per segment: [a+side, a-side, b-side, b+side]? My vertex order per index: i*4+0, i*4+1, i*4+2, i*4+3 with uv (0,i), (1,i), (0,i+1), (1,i+1)... uv I wrote: \n```\nuv[i*6]=0; uv[i*6+1]=i/(n-1);\nuv[i*6+2]=1; uv[i*6+3]=i/(n-1);\nuv[i*6+4]=0; uv[i*6+5]=(i+1)/(n-1);\n```\nThat's 6 floats per segment — but with 4 verts per segment it should be 8 floats (uv pairs for 4 verts). Let me redo cleanly:\n\nPer segment i (0..n-2): 4 vertices: (0,i), (1,i), (0,i+1), (1,i+1) → uv = [0, t, 1, t, 0, t', 1, t'].\nTriangles: 0,2,1 and 1,2,3 (from my idx: `idx.push(i*4, i*4+2, i*4+1, i*4+1, i*4+2, i*4+3)`).\nPositions written: v0 = a+side, v1 = a-side, v2 = b-side, v3 = b+side → consistent with uv (0/1 across).\n\nSo: pos = Float32Array((n-1)*12), uv = Float32Array((n-1)*8), arr.set([9 values?...]) → 12 values: [a+side(3), a-side(3), b-side(3), b+side(3)].\n\nAlso `computeVertexNormals()` every frame is wasteful but ok (small).\n\nAlso the head sample: `this.group.localToWorld(head.set(0, 1.45, -0.25))` — head initial point at back of head; ok.\n\nAudioContext warnings are expected (autoplay policy) — the AudioContext created but suspended until gesture. In the real browser, start happens on click → init() after gesture ✓. In headless there's no real gesture before start (my synthetic click IS a gesture? `b.click()` via JS doesn't count as user activation... it does count as user activation actually — programmatic click() generates a user gesture in Chrome? It treats it as user activation, yes (synthetic clicks don't grant activation though...). Anyway audio.resume in init handles it: if ctx suspended, resume() called on init. But init() only called on startRun. The warnings come from GameAudio construction? No — I create AudioContext lazily in init(). The 6 warnings: audio.init() called by startRun (my test clicked start 3 times? title click, start... whatever) — fine, they're warnings not errors. But wait — in my test, my code clicked #btn-start which called startRun → audio.init → warning because no user activation (programmatic click). Real users clicking will be fine.\n\nBut there were MANY warnings (6) and scarf errors. The scarf error every frame → that's the \"update\" crash — but rendering continued (canvas size read fine). Actually the error was thrown inside player.update → scarf — thrown every frame, meaning `player.update` aborted at scarf each frame → rope/camera/hud never updated! hud time stayed 0:00.00. Right — the exception propagates out of player.update → loop continues (renderer.render happens? No — the exception in player.update propagates up to loop() → aborts loop → renderer.render never runs!). But requestAnimationFrame(loop) was queued at the top of loop() so next frame continues. So each frame: city/street update, then player.update throws → no render, no HUD update. Canvas still had rendered content from title phase (the first frame? no...). Hmm — the title phase rendered. Then after start, every frame throws before render → frozen image of title. That matches \"titleVisible@23s\" from my synthetic click path.\n\nWait, but actually in the test flow: title was shown at ~23s via #btn-start click? No — boot() shows title (that ran fine now? \"titleVisible later: true after 23444 ms\" — that's my click at 23s. Hmm, but boot() should have shown title way earlier (probe settles fast + first content... in headless SwiftShader, tile downloads take a while; the deadline is 7s after createTiles; so title should show ~8-10s after load. The check at 3s showed title hidden, loading visible. Then my loop waited for title up to 20s; titleWaitMs counts total. But the \"click\" evaluate was AFTER the while loop — which exits when title visible OR deadline. Since titleVisible later: true right after the loop, title appeared at some point ≤23s. Could be from boot() at ~10-15s (tile loading slow in software GL) OR from my click. Either way it appeared and then clicking start triggered startRun → scarf crash each frame.\n\nSo main bug = scarf. Fix it now.\n\nAlso, should make updateScarf robust (wrap in try/catch? no — fix properly).\n\nLet me rewrite the scarf part of ninja.ts.",
